In article <1994Apr14.082117.16181@csc.canberra.edu.au>, u914300@student.canberra.edu.au (Mills / Allan William (ISE)) writes:
> Being as we are in the age of fourth generation languages why doesn't 
>someone write a Nethack construction kit. The kit would allow the budding 
>author to design new monsters, design new classes and build their quests 
>levels, design new artifacts,items etc. After designing the kit would add 
>the necessary C code to the original docs ready for compiling. 
>
> Where exactly can copies of the source code be found ? If they aren't to 
>complex then I may have a bash at a construction kit while I'm on 
>holidays. 

Some of that already exists - the special levels and dungeon layout are
described in a special language and anybody can draw and compile his special
level with all the stock and add it tho tyhe game. 

It's more difficult with monsters and items - you have to modify C source 
directly to add them. But: I think that nethack is one of the worst 
candidates for such a project, because of all the multitude of special 
effects and interferences between various monsters/objects/parameters etc
in this game (this is what really distinguishes nethack from all other
'roguelike' stuff). It would be extremely difficult
to build a system that would add the code for such effects to the game,
as it would have not only to add new code, but to modify existing sources
in an infinite number of places... So you'll be probably much better off
choosing some other game for such projects (or even creating a new one 
yourself). 

If you want to look at it, sources are where they always were: 
linc.cis.upenn.edu (and many other sites have them as well).

